<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Pashalik of Yanina</h1><p class="st-meta">1788 CE – 1822 CE · capital Ioannina</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Virtually independent domain of Ali Pasha of Tepelena, who ruled Epirus and much of mainland Greece from Ioannina and dealt with European powers as an equal.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-pasha-of-yanina">Ali Pasha</a>, an Albanian notable from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tepelene">Tepelena</a>, rose through brigandage and Ottoman service, becoming governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trikala">Trikala</a> and then, in the late 1780s, pasha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a>. By force, alliances and intrigue he extended his authority over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/albania">Albania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> and parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, controlling the main routes of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ali ruled from his fortress palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a> through a personal network of officials and Albanian troops, with Greek secretaries and merchants prominent at court. Ioannina became a centre of Greek learning and trade. He negotiated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, entertained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a> in 1809, crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/souliotes">Souliotes</a> and acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parga">Parga</a> in 1819.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii">Mahmud II</a> declared Ali a rebel in 1820 and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a>; Ali was killed in January 1822. The long campaign tied down Ottoman forces just as the Greek War of Independence broke out in 1821, helping the revolt survive.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pashalik-of-yanina.jpg" alt="Pashalik of Yanina" width="320" height="337"><figcaption>Image: N.Hoxha · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albanian_Pashaliks,_1815-1821.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1788 CE – 1822 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ioannina</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ali Pasha of Tepelena</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek, Albanian, Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>, <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pashalik of Ioannina, Ali Pasha's state</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1805&amp;focus=pashalik-of-yanina" aria-label="Pashalik of Yanina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5194 757 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Pashalik of Yanina lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5514 1013L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5514 1013h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5514 1013h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ioannina. <a href="/atlas?year=1805&amp;focus=pashalik-of-yanina">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

